{
  "concept": "The plenoptic function records, at every point in space, the intensity of light arriving from every direction — and a pinhole camera is just a device that makes that function nearly zero except along a few select directions.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "White scene background with a thick black horizontal ground line across the bottom",
    "Green-canopy, brown-trunk cartoon tree on the right side of the scene, sitting on the ground line",
    "Small black outline bird glyph in the upper-right sky",
    "Two free-space sample points in the middle of the scene, drawn as red filled dots (one lower-left, one upper-right of it)",
    "Radial 'starburst' of ~24 black arrows converging inward onto each free-space red dot, evenly spaced in angle, all arrowheads pointing at the dot",
    "Pinhole camera on the left: a black rectangular box outline representing the camera body/image plane",
    "Small gap (pinhole aperture) on the right wall of the camera box",
    "Two red sample points on the interior left wall (image plane) of the camera box — one near the top, one near the bottom",
    "Two long arrows entering through the pinhole and crossing each other, each terminating with an arrowhead on one of the interior red dots (demonstrating image inversion)",
    "Text label 'Pinhole camera' above the camera box",
    "Angular distribution inset: polar plot of intensity L(x, y, θ) versus direction θ for the currently selected sample point",
    "Legend/annotation: dense ring = light from all directions; isolated spikes = light only from aperture directions",
    "Axis labels for the polar inset (θ from 0° to 360°, radius = radiance)",
    "Moving crosshair/marker showing the learner-selected sampling location"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "sampleProbe",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "That the plenoptic function is defined everywhere — clicking any point in the scene yields its own full 360° profile of incoming radiance, and points near the tree or sky look different from points in open air."
    },
    {
      "id": "locationMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Sampling location",
      "options": ["Free space", "Inside pinhole camera"],
      "default": "Free space",
      "teaches": "The contrast between a dense angular distribution in open space (light from all directions) and a sparse one inside the camera (nearly all directions are zero — only rays through the pinhole survive).",
      "state": "default"
    },
    {
      "id": "apertureSize",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Pinhole aperture size",
      "range": [0.02, 1.0, 0.02],
      "default": 0.08,
      "teaches": "Why the pinhole makes imaging possible: shrinking the aperture narrows each interior point's angular support toward a single direction, trading light for a sharp one-ray-per-pixel mapping."
    },
    {
      "id": "rayTrace",
      "type": "animation",
      "label": "Trace rays into the probe",
      "loop": true,
      "teaches": "That each arrow is an actual light ray traveling from a scene surface to the sample point, so the plenoptic value is just 'what you would see looking that way from here'."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "A point in open air",
      "narration": "A red probe sits in free space. Arrows stream in from every direction at once — the plenoptic function at this point is a full, dense 360° profile of radiance.",
      "state": { "locationMode": "Free space" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Move the probe",
      "narration": "Click anywhere in the scene to relocate the probe. Directions pointing at the tree return green radiance, directions pointing at the sky return background — the function varies with both position and direction.",
      "state": { "locationMode": "Free space" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Step inside the camera",
      "narration": "Now the probe sits on the image plane inside the pinhole camera. The starburst collapses: only the narrow cone of directions that passes through the pinhole carries light; everything else is zero.",
      "state": { "locationMode": "Inside pinhole camera", "apertureSize": 0.3 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Shrink the pinhole",
      "narration": "As the aperture closes, the surviving cone narrows to a single direction. Each image-plane point now samples exactly one ray of the plenoptic function — that is what forms a sharp, inverted image.",
      "state": { "locationMode": "Inside pinhole camera", "apertureSize": 0.04 }
    },
    {
      "title": "A camera is a plenoptic sampler",
      "narration": "Watch rays travel from the tree, through the pinhole, to the two interior probes — top of the tree lands low, bottom lands high. A photograph is a thin slice of the plenoptic function, and a NeRF is an attempt to recover the whole thing.",
      "state": { "locationMode": "Inside pinhole camera", "apertureSize": 0.04 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Light in a scene is fully described by one function L(x, y, θ) giving radiance at every point in every direction; a camera doesn't create an image so much as mask that function down to a few nonzero directions per point.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Wide landscape layout, pure white background, thick black ground bar along the bottom edge. Right third: a flat-shaded cartoon tree — bright green lobed canopy, brown trunk with two splayed roots — plus a tiny black outline bird glyph in the upper-right sky. Center: two red dots (lower-left one and an upper-right one), each surrounded by an evenly spaced radial burst of about two dozen thin black arrows all pointing inward toward the dot, arrowheads touching the dot, leaving a small white halo around it. Left third: a tall black-outlined rectangle labeled 'Pinhole camera' in a clean sans-serif above it; the rectangle's right wall has a small gap acting as the pinhole. Inside, two red dots sit on the left (image-plane) wall, one upper and one lower; two long thin arrows enter through the pinhole, cross each other in an X, and terminate with arrowheads on those dots — making the inversion obvious. Interactive additions: a polar inset panel (dark plot on light card) showing radiance versus direction for the active probe — a full smooth ring for free-space probes, a single narrow spike (or two) for in-camera probes; color the polar lobes by what they see (green for tree, pale blue/grey for sky, brown for trunk). Highlight the active probe with a pulsing ring. Keep line weights heavy and the palette limited to black, red, green, brown, white, matching the textbook's flat illustrative style."
}

